},"experimentalSemanticProfile":{"centralFigures":[],"centralPlaces":[{"name":"Little Big Horn","confidence":0.84},{"name":"Wounded Knee","confidence":0.82}],"argument":{"present":true,"statement":"America's original inhabitants were stripped of their land and way of life through broken promises and bloody persecutions.","confidence":0.97},"academicOrientation":{"score":28,"confidence":0.91},"profileConfidence":0.86,"lowerConfidenceCandidates":{"centralFigures":[{"name":"John Ross","confidence":0.52},{"name":"Andrew Jackson","confidence":0.48},{"name":"Red Cloud","confidence":0.46}]},"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"2c9b1c1dabd6d237f70714d2202d1c6973e1ee0f7362e901d89be41c321ddfe2","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"primarySubject":"Race & Ethnicity","secondarySubjects":["American History","Politics & Government"],"confidence":"high","rationale":"The book focuses primarily on the experiences of American Indians and their struggles against dispossession and racial injustices, fitting best within Race & Ethnicity, while also encompassing elements of American History and Politics & Government.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"5e658ef5594df38c5f4753e0a8b376c23000318a56bd862b2264a60211f5cf17","summary":"Marks dramatically illustrates how, across the nation and over the course of nearly four centuries, America's original inhabitants were stripped of both their land and their way of life by a series of broken promises and bloody persecutions. Here, of course, are such well-known events as the Battle of Little Big Horn, the Trail of Tears, and the massacre at Wounded Knee. And here, too, are such equally well-known personalities as Chief Joseph, Geronimo, Cochise, and Andrew Jackson, a president whose perfidies to the Indians still retain the power to shock and dismay. But also recounted are such comparable but less famous episodes as the Navajos' Long Walk of removal from their homelands in the last half of the nineteenth century, the fervent Snake Indian resistance to the allotting of Indian lands early in this century, and the disastrous effects of government dam projects on Indian communities in the 1950s, as well as a discussion of the benefits and draw-backs of legalized gambling on Indian reservations in the past decade. Among the forgotten figures the book brings to life are William McIntosh, who sold the land out from under his fellow Creeks and was subsequently executed by them; John Ross, who led the Cherokee nation throughout its removal and reconstruction, only to see it split apart by the Civil War; and the Oglala Sioux warrior Red Cloud, who forced the U.S.","publicationYear":1998,"subjectCategories":[{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Politics and government","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-in-a-barren-land-american-indian-dispossession-and-survival-paula-mitchell-marks"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Race relations","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-in-a-barren-land-american-indian-dispossession-and-survival-paula-mitchell-marks"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Government policy","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-in-a-barren-land-american-indian-dispossession-and-survival-paula-mitchell-marks"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Government relations","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-in-a-barren-land-american-indian-dispossession-and-survival-paula-mitchell-marks"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Indians of North America","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-in-a-barren-land-american-indian-dispossession-and-survival-paula-mitchell-marks"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Relocation","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-in-a-barren-land-american-indian-dispossession-and-survival-paula-mitchell-marks"},{"sour